<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-Baptiste Bagaza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1946">1946</a>–<a href="/years/2016">2016</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Baptiste Bagaza was a Burundian army officer who seized power in a coup in November 1976 and ruled as president until 1987. He pursued development projects but clashed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, restricting religious activity and expelling missionaries. He was overthrown by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-buyoya">Pierre Buyoya</a> while abroad in September 1987.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-bagaza.jpg" alt="Jean-Baptiste Bagaza" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Louis Debaize · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Baptiste_Bagaza_-_1978.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bagaza was born in Rutovu, Bururi Province, in Belgian-ruled Ruanda-Urundi on 29 August 1946, into the Hima, part of the wider Tutsi ethnic group. After Catholic schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Bujumbura</a> he enlisted in the army of the newly independent Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a>. He was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> in 1966, studied at the Royal Military Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> until 1971 and earned a sociology degree. Back in Burundi in 1972, he was involved in President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-micombero">Michel Micombero</a>'s killings of ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> that year, though the extent or nature of his involvement remains unclear. In November 1972 he became lieutenant colonel and chief of the general staff.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Bagaza overthrew Micombero in a military coup on 1 November 1976, and a 30-member <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a>, the Supreme Revolutionary Council, declared him president on 10 November, when he was thirty. He attacked corruption, reformed taxation and administration, and made modest reforms to improve conditions for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a>. The feudal system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a>, the Ubugererwa, was abolished in 1977, as was the ikori poll tax, and this earned him goodwill among the Hutu. Officially he banned all mention of ethnicity, but the political scientist René Lemarchand and the researcher Nigel Watt argued that this disguised the continued dominance of the Tutsi elite. From 1976 to 1979 the Council consisted only of Tutsi officers.</p><p>His programme of economic modernisation included two new hydroelectric dams, road building, drinking water and a port on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>. A constitution of 1981 consolidated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-for-national-progress">UPRONA</a>, and in the election of 1984 he was re-elected with 99.6 per cent of the vote. From 1977-78 he conducted an anti-clerical campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, which grew into a military operation after the 1984 election: foreign missionaries were expelled, Catholic media and church services banned, Church schools nationalised, Church figures arrested and tortured, and 87 churches closed, among them Gitega Cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In September 1987 Major Pierre Buyoya led a coup while Bagaza was abroad in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. Bagaza went into exile in Uganda and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. In 1994 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> and founded the Party for National Recovery, PARENA, which opposed power-sharing with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> factions. In January 1997 he was placed under house arrest for gathering weapons for a plot against Buyoya. He stepped down as head of PARENA in March 2014. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> on 4 May 2016, aged 69, and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Bujumbura</a> on 17 May. His infrastructure and development projects remained well remembered in Burundi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jean-Baptiste Bagaza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Further coups brought <b>Jean-Baptiste Bagaza</b> in 1976 and Pierre Buyoya in 1987.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-burundi">Republic of Burundi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rutovu, in what is now <a href="/countries/burundi">Burundi</a>. Died in Brussels, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-3.89,29.85&amp;year=1991" aria-label="Rutovu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5378 2309 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jean-Baptiste Bagaza"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5828 2579h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5828 2579h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-3.89,29.85&amp;year=1991">What was here in 1991? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
